---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro'

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')

const authors = [
	{ name: 'Kang Minkyu', href: 'https://mk040412.github.io/' },
]

const links = [
	{ label: 'Project Weasel', href: 'https://mk040412.github.io/Project-Weasel/' },
	{ label: 'Project Slide', href: 'https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1j5RRUXX4L8SYnvOt--nQJJUP3YI-ml_C6bIDvD-2vtI/edit?usp=sharing' },
]

// Qualitative scenario clips (lossless renders compressed for the web).
const clip = (name) => `${base}/clips/${name}.mp4`

// Paper figures (exported from the design/architecture drawings).
const fig = (name) => `${base}/figures/${name}.png`

// Scenario groups, ordered as a story: what works → coordination →
// human-in-the-loop → and why the framework is needed (failure cases).
const works = [
	{ src: 'different_tasks', label: 'Eight agents split into different jobs and work in parallel.' },
	{ src: 'log_gathering_a', label: 'Chopping logs to stock shared materials.' },
	{ src: 'log_gathering_b', label: 'Gathering keeps the supply chain moving.' },
]
const failures = [
	{ src: 'farming_stuck_farm', label: 'Even when the farm task is assigned directly by command, without the coordination framework the agent stalls and never completes it.' },
	{ src: 'farming_stuck_resource', label: 'Even given every material needed to farm, the agent never manages to get ready to start farming, no matter how long it is left.' },
]
---

<Layout
	title='MineMate: Multi-Agent Emergent Capability with Humans'
	pageTitle='MineMate'
	description='Sub-project of Project Weasel: emergent multi-agent capability with humans in the loop, in the open-ended world of Minecraft.'
	authors={authors}
	links={links}
	heroVideo={clip('open_ended')}
	heroAlt='An open-ended Minecraft world'
	faviconHref={`${base}/minemate.png`}
>
	<h1>Abstract</h1>
	<p>
		<b>MineMate</b> studies what happens when several people and many LLM-driven agents team up in a
		shared, open-ended Minecraft world. In a main experiment, <b>four participants shared one world</b>
		and each directed their <b>own team of agents</b> while I swept the <b>Human:Agent ratio from 1:1 to
		1:16</b> on two subtasks, gathering <b>logs</b> and crafting a <b>stone pickaxe</b>. Individual agents
		were capable; the bottleneck was <b>coordination</b>. Per-team yield rose with the agent count only
		for work that splits into independent, parallel effort (logs), and <b>saturated almost immediately</b>
		for the collaborative recipe (stone pickaxe), whose bottleneck is inter-agent coordination. Given a
		free choice, people did not maximize autonomy: they settled on small <b>Decoupled</b> agent groups
		with a <b>Centralized</b> (one-coordinator) interaction, observed everything through <b>text (Slack)</b>,
		and spent their time <b>directing agents and clearing bottlenecks</b> rather than playing. The takeaway:
		embodied LLM agents are <b>strong as singletons but weak at collaboration</b>, and the human's role is
		becoming that of a <b>coordinator of emergent, only-partially-legible teammates</b>.
	</p>
	<p class='report-link'>
		📄 Full write-up:
		<a href={`${base}/MineMate Report.pdf`} download>MineMate Report (PDF)</a>.
	</p>

	<h1>Overview</h1>
	<ul>
		<li><b>MineMate</b> is a sub-project of <a href='https://mk040412.github.io/Project-Weasel/'>Project Weasel</a>: a study of <b>what phenomena emerge when multiple humans and LLM agents cooperate</b>, building, farming, and gathering together in a shared, open-ended Minecraft world.</li>
		<li>I study whether useful capability can <b>emerge</b> from many language-model agents acting in a shared world, and how a coordination framework plus a human-in-the-loop make that emergence reliable.</li>
	</ul>

	<h1>Why Minecraft?</h1>
	<p>
		Minecraft is an <b>open-ended, partially-observable sandbox</b>: there is no fixed objective, each
		agent only sees its local surroundings, and goals must be discovered rather than scripted. That makes
		it a strong testbed for <b>multi-agent emergence</b>: capability has to arise from the agents'
		interaction with the world and with each other, not from hand-written rules. The clip above is the
		raw world the agents wake up into.
	</p>

	<h1>Partial observation matters</h1>
	<p>
		An embodied agent only ever sees its own <b>first-person view</b>. From that egocentric vantage
		(left) it often <b>cannot tell how much it has actually accomplished</b>, or what is right beside
		it. The <b>same moment in a third-person 3D view</b> (right) tells a different story: the agent
		has in fact done a great deal of work. Because the agent is constrained to this <b>partial, 3D
		first-person observation</b> of the world, it cannot always judge its own situation correctly: a
		core difficulty of acting under <b>partial observability</b>.
		<span class='muted'>Click either video to enlarge.</span>
	</p>
	<div class='compare'>
		<figure class='cmpcard'>
			<video src={clip('partial_observation')} loop muted playsinline preload='none'></video>
			<figcaption>First-person view: all the agent actually sees.</figcaption>
		</figure>
		<figure class='cmpcard'>
			<video src={clip('partial_observation_3d')} loop muted playsinline preload='none'></video>
			<figcaption>Third-person 3D view: the work the agent really did.</figcaption>
		</figure>
	</div>

	<h1>What the agents do well</h1>
	<p>
		Given a concrete sub-task, each agent performs reliably. The eight agents naturally <b>divide labor</b>
		(logging, mining, and crafting tools) and run those jobs in parallel without colliding.
	</p>
	<div class='clip-grid'>
		{works.map((c) => (
			<figure class='clip'>
				<video src={clip(c.src)} loop muted playsinline preload='none'></video>
				<figcaption>{c.label}</figcaption>
			</figure>
		))}
	</div>

	<h1>Agents coordinate to share resources</h1>
	<p>
		Capability is not just per-agent. It is <b>social</b>. Agents communicate over shared channels to
		request and hand off items. Here an agent needs a crafting table; it asks <b>Nova</b>, and Nova
		brings one over. The exchange emerges from their dialogue, not from a hard-coded trade.
	</p>
	<figure class='clip clip-wide'>
		<video src={clip('nova_communication')} loop muted playsinline preload='none'></video>
		<figcaption>An agent asks Nova for a crafting table, and Nova delivers it.</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<h1>Humans and agents team up</h1>
	<p>
		With a <b>human in the loop</b>, the agents take direction and contribute to larger projects. This
		building session shows that human–agent teaming is workable: the human sets intent, and the agents
		help realize it together.
	</p>
	<figure class='clip clip-wide'>
		<video src={clip('human_agent_building')} loop muted playsinline preload='none'></video>
		<figcaption>Human and agents building together.</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<h1>Why the framework matters</h1>
	<p>
		My contribution is the <b>coordination framework</b>. Remove it and the <i>same</i> model gets
		<b>stuck</b>: it talks instead of acting, loops on a sub-goal, or cannot recover from a missing
		resource. The two farming attempts below fail exactly where the framework would have kept the team
		unblocked, motivating why the structure is needed.
	</p>
	<div class='clip-grid'>
		{failures.map((c) => (
			<figure class='clip'>
				<video src={clip(c.src)} loop muted playsinline preload='none'></video>
				<figcaption>{c.label}</figcaption>
			</figure>
		))}
	</div>

	<hr />
	<h1>Design space</h1>
	<p>
		Beyond the demos, MineMate is studied as an <b>HCI problem</b>: how one human
		coordinates with <i>many</i> agents at once. Single-agent capability is already strong.
		The hard part is <b>teaming</b>. <span class='muted'>Click the figure to enlarge.</span>
	</p>

	<figure class='fig-wide'>
		<img loading='lazy' src={fig('design_space')} alt='MineMate design space' />
		<figcaption>
			<b>Design space.</b> I partition human–agent teaming along three axes:
			<b>model communication</b> (coupled → decoupled → isolated → unstructured group
			structures), <b>observability</b> (partial in-game vs. full in text), and
			<b>user–model interaction</b> (direct, hierarchical, decentralized talking systems).
			Each cell is measured for efficiency and interaction preference.
		</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<h1>Coordinating through Slack</h1>
	<div class='slack-block'>
		<div class='slack-media'>
			<img class='slack-img' loading='lazy' src={fig('slack')} alt='Slack interface for MineMate' />
			<video class='slack-vid' src={clip('slack_use')} controls muted preload='metadata' playsinline></video>
		</div>
		<p class='slack-text'>
			I added a <b>Slack interface</b> so that <b>users can coordinate with one another</b>
			easily (and command the agents) from <i>outside</i> the text-flooded in-game view.
			Each agent gets its own avatar and sub-channel, so a team of people can divide work and
			follow what every agent is doing in one place. <span class='muted'>Press ▶ on the clip to
			watch a short walkthrough.</span>
		</p>
	</div>

	<hr />
	<h1>Results: capability scales, but with diminishing returns</h1>
	<p>
		In a small main experiment, <b>four participants shared a single world</b> for a <b>~5-hour</b>
		session. After choosing their preferred configuration during free play, the measured runs were
		<b>unified</b> for everyone to the most-preferred setup, <b>Decoupled</b> agent groups with a
		<b>Centralized</b> (one-coordinator) interaction, with agents driven by <b>Gemma</b> and
		<b>Kimi&nbsp;2.5</b> in parallel. Each person directed <b>only their own team</b> and never took
		over someone else's agents, though the <b>agents themselves could message and request work across
		teams</b> (one team's agent could ask another person's agent for help). I then swept each team's
		<b>Human:Agent ratio</b> from <b>1:1 to 1:16</b>, with a fresh world per run and each
		<b>25&nbsp;min</b> run split into <b>15&nbsp;min gathering logs</b> then <b>10&nbsp;min crafting a
		stone pickaxe</b>. Notably, people <b>mostly did not play directly</b>: they told agents what to do
		and spent their time <b>managing bottlenecks</b>, unblocking stuck agents and re-allocating work,
		so the human's role looked more like a coordinator than a player. More agents help, but with
		<b>diminishing marginal returns</b>, and how fast the benefit runs out depends on the task.
		<span class='muted'>Click the figure to enlarge.</span>
	</p>
	<figure class='fig-wide'>
		<img loading='lazy' src={fig('resource_scaling')} alt='Per-team resource yield vs. Human:Agent ratio for logs and stone pickaxe' />
		<figcaption>
			<b>Diminishing returns from more agents.</b> <b>Per-team</b> yield (one person and the agents
			they direct, averaged over the four teams) versus team size, for the two subtasks.
			<b>Log gathering</b> is abundant and embarrassingly parallel, so it keeps climbing across the
			whole sweep. The <b>stone pickaxe</b>, a multi-step <i>collaborative</i> recipe, saturates
			almost immediately (flat past 1:4), because its bottleneck is inter-agent coordination,
			exactly where the agents are weakest. Capability grows with the agent count only as far as a
			task <b>decomposes into independent, parallel work</b>.
		</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<hr />
	<h1>Conclusion</h1>
	<ul>
		<li>Embodied LLM agents are <b>strong as singletons but weak at collaboration</b>: each agent
			follows free-form instructions well, yet the work breaks down at the <b>seams</b> between
			agents.</li>
		<li>Adding agents follows a <b>law of diminishing marginal utility</b>, and the benefit
			disappears as soon as a task needs agents to cooperate rather than run in parallel.</li>
		<li>Given a free choice, users settle on a <b>middle band of agency</b>: small <i>decoupled</i>
			groups that share only what they need and never carry two roles, watched through <b>text</b>
			(Slack) rather than the in-world view.</li>
		<li>As agents move into the physical world, the central HCI problem is <b>keeping a person in
			control of a team of emergent, only-partially-legible teammates</b>, which is what
			<b>MineMate</b> is built to study.</li>
	</ul>

	<hr />
	<h1>Acknowledgement</h1>
	<p>
		Thank you to the UNIST Minecraft Community, my high-school friends, my UNIST
		friends, my Mobile Computing teammates (the greatest I've ever met), and Google's
		TPU Research Cloud for supporting the experiments.
	</p>

	<div id='lightbox' class='lightbox' role='dialog' aria-label='enlarged clip'>
		<img id='lightbox-img' src='' alt='' />
		<video id='lightbox-vid' loop muted playsinline controls></video>
	</div>
</Layout>

<style>
	.clip-grid {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr));
		gap: 16px;
		margin: 16px 0 8px;
	}
	.clip {
		margin: 0;
	}
	.clip video {
		width: 100%;
		height: auto;
		display: block;
		border-radius: 6px;
		background: #000;
	}
	.clip figcaption {
		color: var(--muted);
		font-size: 14px;
		margin-top: 6px;
		line-height: 1.4;
		text-align: center;
	}
	.clip-wide {
		max-width: 800px;
		margin: 16px auto 8px;
	}

	/* Side-by-side comparison (first-person vs 3D view) */
	.compare {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
		gap: 16px;
		margin: 16px 0 8px;
	}
	.cmpcard {
		margin: 0;
	}
	.cmpcard video {
		width: 100%;
		height: auto;
		display: block;
		border-radius: 6px;
		background: #000;
	}
	.cmpcard figcaption {
		color: var(--muted);
		font-size: 14px;
		margin-top: 6px;
		line-height: 1.4;
		text-align: center;
	}

	/* Click any clip to enlarge */
	.clip video,
	.cmpcard video {
		cursor: zoom-in;
		transition: transform 0.12s ease;
	}
	.clip video:hover,
	.cmpcard video:hover {
		transform: scale(1.02);
	}

	/* Click-to-enlarge lightbox */
	.lightbox {
		position: fixed;
		inset: 0;
		background: rgba(10, 16, 28, 0.88);
		display: none;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: center;
		z-index: 1000;
		cursor: zoom-out;
		padding: 24px;
	}
	.lightbox.open {
		display: flex;
	}
	.lightbox video,
	.lightbox img {
		max-width: 92vw;
		max-height: 92vh;
		width: auto;
		height: auto;
		border-radius: 10px;
		box-shadow: 0 10px 60px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.6);
	}
	.lightbox img {
		background: #fff;
	}
	#lightbox-img {
		display: none;
	}

	/* Design-space + system figures */
	.fig-wide {
		margin: 18px 0 8px;
	}
	.fig-wide img {
		width: 100%;
		height: auto;
		display: block;
		border-radius: 8px;
		border: 1px solid var(--border);
		background: #fff;
	}
	.fig-wide figcaption {
		color: var(--muted);
		font-size: 14px;
		margin-top: 8px;
		line-height: 1.5;
	}
	/* Slack interface block: media (png + click-to-play video) on the left,
	   text on the right; media height-capped so the tall portrait assets
	   don't make the section overly tall. */
	.slack-block {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: auto 1fr;
		gap: 22px;
		align-items: center;
		margin: 16px 0 8px;
	}
	.slack-media {
		display: flex;
		gap: 12px;
		align-items: flex-start;
	}
	.slack-media .slack-img,
	.slack-media .slack-vid {
		height: 340px;
		width: auto;
		max-width: 100%;
		display: block;
		border-radius: 8px;
		border: 1px solid var(--border);
		background: #000;
	}
	.slack-media .slack-img {
		background: #fff;
		cursor: zoom-in;
		transition: transform 0.12s ease;
	}
	.slack-media .slack-img:hover {
		transform: scale(1.02);
	}
	.slack-media .slack-vid {
		cursor: pointer;
	}
	.slack-text {
		color: var(--muted);
		font-size: 15px;
		line-height: 1.6;
	}
	/* Click the design-space / Slack figure to enlarge */
	.fig-wide img {
		cursor: zoom-in;
		transition: transform 0.12s ease;
	}

	@media (max-width: 700px) {
		.compare {
			grid-template-columns: 1fr;
		}
		.slack-block {
			grid-template-columns: 1fr;
		}
		.slack-media .slack-img,
		.slack-media .slack-vid {
			height: 260px;
		}
	}
</style>

<script>
	const lb = document.getElementById('lightbox')
	const lbVid = document.getElementById('lightbox-vid') as HTMLVideoElement | null
	const lbImg = document.getElementById('lightbox-img') as HTMLImageElement | null
	if (lb && lbVid && lbImg) {
		const openVid = (src: string) => {
			lbImg.style.display = 'none'
			lbImg.removeAttribute('src')
			lbVid.style.display = 'block'
			lbVid.setAttribute('src', src)
			lb.classList.add('open')
			const p = lbVid.play()
			if (p && p.catch) p.catch(() => {})
		}
		const openImg = (src: string, alt: string) => {
			lbVid.pause()
			lbVid.removeAttribute('src')
			lbVid.style.display = 'none'
			lbImg.style.display = 'block'
			lbImg.setAttribute('src', src)
			lbImg.setAttribute('alt', alt)
			lb.classList.add('open')
		}
		const close = () => {
			lb.classList.remove('open')
			lbVid.pause()
			lbVid.removeAttribute('src')
			lbImg.removeAttribute('src')
		}
		document.querySelectorAll('.clip video, .cmpcard video').forEach((v) => {
			v.addEventListener('click', () => openVid(v.getAttribute('src') || ''))
		})
		document.querySelectorAll('.fig-wide img, .slack-img').forEach((im) => {
			im.addEventListener('click', () => openImg(im.getAttribute('src') || '', im.getAttribute('alt') || ''))
		})
		lb.addEventListener('click', close)
		document.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
			if (e.key === 'Escape') close()
		})
	}

	// Play only the videos near/in the viewport so the browser never decodes
	// ~10 clips at once (that simultaneous decoding is what makes playback stutter).
	const vids = document.querySelectorAll('.clip video, .cmpcard video')
	if ('IntersectionObserver' in window) {
		const io = new IntersectionObserver(
			(entries) => {
				entries.forEach((e) => {
					const v = e.target as HTMLVideoElement
					if (e.isIntersecting) {
						if (v.preload !== 'auto') v.preload = 'auto'
						const p = v.play()
						if (p && p.catch) p.catch(() => {})
					} else {
						v.pause()
					}
				})
			},
			{ rootMargin: '200px 0px', threshold: 0.25 }
		)
		vids.forEach((v) => io.observe(v))
	} else {
		// Fallback: no IntersectionObserver, just autoplay everything.
		vids.forEach((v) => {
			const el = v as HTMLVideoElement
			el.preload = 'auto'
			const p = el.play()
			if (p && p.catch) p.catch(() => {})
		})
	}
</script>
